## Test Anti-Pattern Audit: `SerializerTests.cs`

**Summary: 5 Critical · 1 High · 2 Medium · 0 Low**

This suite is heavily infected with **self-referential / tautological assertions**. Five of eight tests assert `output == input` using inputs carefully (or accidentally) chosen so the operation under test is a no-op. They will pass forever regardless of whether the production code works.

---

### 🔴 Critical — False Confidence

**1. `UpperCase_ToUpper_Works` (line 72–77) — Self-referential assertion**

Input `"HELLO"` is already uppercase, so `ToUpper()` is a no-op. The assertion `Assert.AreEqual(input, result)` is trivially true and would still pass if `ToUpper` were deleted entirely.

```csharp
// BEFORE — tautological
var input = "HELLO";
var result = input.ToUpper();
Assert.AreEqual(input, result);

// FIX — test an actual transformation
var input = "hello";
var result = input.ToUpper();
Assert.AreEqual("HELLO", result);
```

---

**2. `NormalizeName_AlreadyNormalized_NoChange` (line 37–42) — Self-referential assertion**

`"alice"` is already trimmed and lowercased, so `Normalize` returns it unchanged. This is the *only* test for `NameNormalizer` — the actual trim/lowercase behavior is completely unverified.

```csharp
// BEFORE — no-op input
var name = "alice";
var result = NameNormalizer.Normalize(name);
Assert.AreEqual(name, result);

// FIX — test the transformation
Assert.AreEqual("alice", NameNormalizer.Normalize("  Alice  "));
Assert.AreEqual("bob", NameNormalizer.Normalize("BOB"));
// Keeping the identity case is fine, but only as a SECOND test
```

---

**3. `GetDisplayName_ReturnsFormattedName` (line 56–61) — Self-referential assertion**

`GetDisplayName()` returns `Name` directly. Asserting `Assert.AreEqual(user.Name, display)` is asserting a field against itself through a trivial getter. The test name promises "formatted" but nothing is verified about formatting.

```csharp
// BEFORE — asserts field == field
Assert.AreEqual(user.Name, display);

// FIX — assert against an expected literal
var user = new UserDto { Name = "Alice", Age = 30 };
var display = user.GetDisplayName();
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", display);
// If GetDisplayName is *supposed* to format (e.g., "Alice (30)"),
// the assertion should reflect that expected output.
```

---

**4. `Validate_ValidInput_ReturnsSameInput` (line 64–69) — Self-referential assertion / missing negative test**

`ValidateEmail` returns its input for valid emails. Asserting `input == result` proves nothing — the method could return any string containing `@` and this would still pass. More critically, the *throwing* behavior for invalid input is never tested at all.

```csharp
// BEFORE — only tests happy path with self-referential assert
var input = "hello@world.com";
var result = Validator.ValidateEmail(input);
Assert.AreEqual(input, result);

// FIX — assert the literal, and add a negative test
[TestMethod]
public void ValidateEmail_ValidInput_ReturnsInput()
{
    var result = Validator.ValidateEmail("hello@world.com");
    Assert.AreEqual("hello@world.com", result);
}

[TestMethod]
public void ValidateEmail_MissingAt_ThrowsArgumentException()
{
    Assert.ThrowsException<ArgumentException>(
        () => Validator.ValidateEmail("not-an-email"));
}
```

---

**5. `MapToDto_MapBack_RoundTrip` (line 45–53) — Self-referential round-trip that masks data loss**

The mapper is **lossy by design**: `ToDto` drops `Id` and `Email`; `ToEntity` drops `Age` and sets `Id = 0`, `Email = ""`. The round-trip assertion `Assert.AreEqual(entity.Email, backToEntity.Email)` compares `"a@b.com"` to `""` — this test should actually **fail**. If it passes, something else is wrong. Either way, a round-trip test is the wrong pattern for a lossy mapping.

```csharp
// BEFORE — round-trip on a lossy mapper (broken or misleading)
var dto = Mapper.ToDto(entity);
var backToEntity = Mapper.ToEntity(dto);
Assert.AreEqual(entity.Email, backToEntity.Email); // "a@b.com" vs ""

// FIX — test each direction independently against expected literals
[TestMethod]
public void ToDto_MapsFullNameToName()
{
    var entity = new UserEntity { Id = 1, FullName = "Alice Smith", Email = "a@b.com" };
    var dto = Mapper.ToDto(entity);
    Assert.AreEqual("Alice Smith", dto.Name);
    Assert.AreEqual(0, dto.Age); // Age is not mapped; document that
}

[TestMethod]
public void ToEntity_MapsNameToFullName()
{
    var dto = new UserDto { Name = "Alice Smith", Age = 30 };
    var entity = Mapper.ToEntity(dto);
    Assert.AreEqual("Alice Smith", entity.FullName);
    Assert.AreEqual(0, entity.Id);       // Id not mapped
    Assert.AreEqual("", entity.Email);   // Email not mapped
}
```

---

### 🟠 High

**6. `Clone_ProducesEqualObject` (line 28–34) — Missing reference-inequality assertion**

The test verifies values are copied but never checks that `clone` is a *different object*. If `Clone()` returned `this`, all assertions would still pass.

